<?php

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Models\User;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Response;

class UserController extends Controller
{
    public function show($id)
    {
        // Fetch the user from the database by their ID
        $user = User::findOrFail($id);

        // Prepare the data to be sent as JSON
        $data = [
            'id' => $user->id,
            'name' => $user->name,
            'email' => $user->email,
            // Add other user details as needed
        ];

        // Return the user data as JSON
        return Response::json($data);
    }
}
```

This function, `show`, is part of a `UserController`. It accepts an ID as a parameter, finds the user with that ID in the database using the `findOrFail` method, prepares a JSON-ready data structure, and then returns the JSON response using the `Response::json` method. You'll need to adjust the code according to your project's specific requirements and database schema.

